#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175820 :question/id #uuid "616e03ca-b0fc-53f1-baf0-8034d7731891" :question/math-academy-id "e-8885" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 4\\sin{t}, \\: z \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 4\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\frac{16{sin}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\cos{t}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175822 :question/id #uuid "3e65aff0-a768-58fd-b41c-ca38ce3eb0bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8888" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,y) = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: \\text{___} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $2x^2=z.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,2{x}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} 2x^2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175823 :question/id #uuid "3c0c59e2-ceaa-54ab-bb56-3cb6daadb5f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8889" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,y) = \\big \\langle 4\\tan{t}, \\, y, \\, \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,y)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\dfrac{z^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{16} = 1$ for $z \\geq 2.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola $\\dfrac{z^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{16} = 1$ for $z \\geq 2$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x , z \\rangle = \\big \\langle 4\\tan{t}, \\, 2\\sec{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\n\\qquad t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{sec}^{2}⁡t}{4}-\\frac{16{tan}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y,\\,2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$ and $y \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\sec{t}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82401ace-c226-55ca-a5ef-b2e14e6c7e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cosh t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173405} {:answer/id #uuid "ba92dba1-602b-58ac-b8f4-1c295e03bc71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173406} {:answer/id #uuid "bd40b8af-bb6c-5853-82d3-8cfcb0cb65fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173407} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d2715b-fe19-5704-96cd-0396e7d21c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sinh t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173408} {:answer/id #uuid "1624dc22-76b6-5d4f-8414-753e14dc0f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173409}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f7497f-0839-50b7-addb-6102a75d75ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173404}] :question/id #uuid "700c7d88-ca90-5a51-8c35-1157cc4b950b" :question/math-academy-id "q-103980" :question/problem "$f(t, z) = ⟨2\\cos t, _____, z⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t, z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$. What is the second component of $f$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92c9230f-2b9f-51e3-9538-10bd091526c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173411} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a35d31-72bf-5f4e-b5a6-6a58107f6699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173412} {:answer/id #uuid "1684003c-ff41-563f-a3cf-65d230f52f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\tan t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173413} {:answer/id #uuid "48a1f2d5-f114-50f5-87b9-26b1a552c875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173414} {:answer/id #uuid "70d8186