 #uuid "0a20c6d2-699b-56bb-8123-fa833c0c85c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6c9aca83-9ce8-55b9-8ced-bafcb2d3c448" :question/math-academy-id "q-103998" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 3.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 3$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-(3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =9({sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176400 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8976344d-d838-502e-b4be-975fe82dfee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174659} {:answer/id #uuid "ae331b4a-290d-5d0f-b08f-3fd871fb6ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\csc t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174660} {:answer/id #uuid "be3e7c50-063f-5d75-a9b8-b12f02b3bc5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\csc t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174661} {:answer/id #uuid "c85348eb-7e75-5e90-81dd-82a2d5e78b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\tan t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174662} {:answer/id #uuid "431a2779-b83a-5e9b-8edd-ecf661b3dc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\tan t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174663}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c58c139c-c01e-5255-91f7-33ebf0b26761" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4172de1d-e175-5c7f-99ca-9a107259ec3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164387" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle f(x, t) = ⟨x, _____, 2\\sec t⟩, t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x, t)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $3z^{2} - 4y^{2} = 12$ for $z \\ge 2$. What is the second component of $f$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8475316c-8579-5579-ba08-41d5bb64d787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8475316c-8579-5579-ba08-41d5bb64d787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362488} :answer-field/id #uuid "795f305e-67f0-5314-a428-976bafef3452" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5ad7c087-31de-5f2c-99f2-5a1c344721d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-167887" :question/problem "A parametrization of the cylindrical heater is $f(\\theta ,z)=⟨$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,$ where $(\\theta ,z)\\in C.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the circle\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1\n$$\n\nin the $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{9}+\\frac{9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{9} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(\\theta ,z)\\in C$ and $C=[0,2\\pi )\\times [0,3].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cd7a5cc-e659-592c-a30b-217070eb9303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365400} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b3bca3-dd34-5e25-8b55-b30c529cf2e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365401} {:answer/id #uuid "e0752f0b-28bf-552a-942b-e214de9ab9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365402} {:answer/id #uuid "f18bb6d3-2a5a-5f60-a6b3-fbdb6f7c77de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365403} {:answer/id #uuid "2de5b223-c382-5f40-913c-8b71b1f58c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cd7a5cc-e659-592c-a30b-217070eb9303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365400} :answer-field/id #uuid "4849f67e-73d4-59bc-b3d3-b0bcc5c71e3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "96adb906-7cb9-5606-a5fd-4a0f9d2808bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-164440" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,z)=⟨x,\\,6{x}^{2},\\,\\text{___}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $y=6{x}^{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,6{x}^{2},\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $z.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365405 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ec9f457-0a46-5786-943d-980831075831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365407} {:answer/id #uuid "0a987a72-f9a0-5a68-ba03-99fd4a6f8f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365408} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe46c52-aa0a-51fc-9267-b1401f5c53e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365409} {:answer/id #uuid "1510ed05-ccb6-5631-b10e-d3c6917da702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365410} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba30c13-8746-5d21-9cf7-d2bab32d7c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ec9f457-0a46-5786-943d-980831075831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365407} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0ab6088-4e94-502b-9bef-8ea9ffa9fd16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "903e7168-95b7-5bad-a739-c6d09fdecd73" :question/math-academy-id "q-164443" :question/problem "$$\nf(y,z)=⟨\\text{___},\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x=3{y}^{2}.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{y}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $3{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d468ab70-401d-5a96-a5d9-10066682c534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365414} {:answer/id #uuid "29853ec6-e5eb-5f87-8737-fa642421e99f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365415} {:answer/id #uuid "562cf4d7-5216-576b-ab41-c7b6c344e636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365416} {:answer/id #uuid "5e10a9e5-20c8-571e-8fd6-6178d54ecf06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365417} {:answer/id #uuid "06c3d2ac-5e98-5dde-9474-4bc695d82f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{csc}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29853ec6-e5eb-5f87-8737-fa642421e99f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365415} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fc855bc-b950-554d-a402-a0a3bda2d624" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "039615d1-3b91-5876-8c81-da27ba9dec92" :question/math-academy-id "q-164457" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,t)=⟨x,\\,\\text{_____},\\,7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,t)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=49$ for $y\\ge 7.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=49$ for $y\\ge 7$ in $yz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨y,z⟩=⟨7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =(7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-(7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =49{sec}^{2}⁡t-49{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =49({sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,t) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4207d3eb-db6b-589a-b486-b94a7c9598a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365421} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac48455-a05b-5a97-a250-069cc1619393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365422} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7faea4-f222-5378-b934-523cde58c436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365423} {:answer/id #uuid "cdadcbb0-995a-56d8-80a6-25c096de8d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365424} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd196ef-bf7a-5f13-a3cc-fc6226a49d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4207d3eb-db6b-589a-b486-b94a7c9598a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365421} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bb20991-ac0a-5616-8a70-0419a9707880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c46d3a87-a39a-50a3-a78d-09833afff4e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-164519" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,y)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,y,\\,4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,y)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ for $x\\ge 1.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ for $x\\ge 1$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-\\frac{16{tan}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,y,\\,4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fce6433b-566b-5373-9f60-54afb5810670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365428} {:answer/id #uuid "878c8cef-35cb-51a6-b38b-2b4f75ac5495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365429} {:answer/id #uuid "7c0a4702-a64f-517a-b849-f468974a48c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365430} {:answer/id #uuid "13a1e5a6-e5dd-557e-99e3-9aff0e9c76d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365431} {:answer/id #uuid "aade5251-2de5-58c4-8f34-8bc109e8b273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "878c8cef-35cb-51a6-b38b-2b4f75ac5495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365429} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e118899-8a0e-5b48-82c5-20aecfe54e53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9673b35d-7ab7-5053-8bde-0eee9a8197b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-164526" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=25.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1\n$$\n\nin $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{25}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57deffd8-e545-55f1-abe6-d4f07da38672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365435} {:answer/id #uuid "97214db6-700f-5149-8a8a-bfcdaa78370c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365436} {:answer/id #uuid "6053ecc2-43ad-511c-a23d-90c7de3c0861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365437} {:answer/id #uuid "63bb069f-8fe9-576c-b784-794cce9e2f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365438} {:answer/id #uuid "834d7cec-cbed-5efe-bd29-15484716878c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "834d7cec-cbed-5efe-bd29-15484716878c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365439} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd7340ee-5e79-5612-8e06-781561cbe7a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c053e26-9cc8-557f-ade7-75bec2ed7dea" :question/math-academy-id "q-164530" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,y)=⟨\\text{_____},y,\\,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=16.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$\n\nin $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e83a2b2d-5277-5f92-aa24-babf0be055ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365442} {:answer/id #uuid "56c5385c-0ed1-5c6e-a60b-b0dbdd5b7d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365443} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfd544b-056e-57ec-8a1c-f726d0f6a322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365444} {:answer/id #uuid "37afd173-73ed-540e-9cb3-0cdb79d64880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365445} {:answer/id #uuid "187f9c55-a141-5a67-9004-71a8fa64ec6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37afd173-73ed-540e-9cb3-0cdb79d64880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 175921